1
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:06,413
Many rows in, one figure out.

2
00:00:07,213 --> 00:00:16,650
By default, aggregators run after the groups. They fold each group's rows into one figure, and they can read fields, features and attribute labels.

3
00:00:17,450 --> 00:00:25,799
When an aggregation reads a window label, that order flips: the windows run first, over the records, and the aggregation reads their column.

4
00:00:26,599 --> 00:00:35,588
Each aggregation names a type and a field, and optionally a label. Without a label, the output column is the type and the field joined together.

5
00:00:36,388 --> 00:00:41,985
Without groups you get one row for the whole cohort. With a grouper, you get one row per bucket.

6
00:00:42,785 --> 00:00:53,289
Leave out the type and Pulse picks one from the field: a sum for numbers, a modal count for categories, and set frequency for multi-selects. Dates are never defaulted.

7
00:00:54,089 --> 00:00:57,937
Ten members survived the filters. One of them has no spend.

8
00:00:58,737 --> 00:01:05,209
Count counts non-null values only, so it says nine, not ten. Null count reports the one it skipped.

9
00:01:06,009 --> 00:01:13,100
Distinct count counts different values. Frequency counts one value you name, here North, and also gives its share.

10
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:23,209
Mode count is the size of the commonest value, and mode is the value itself. On a tie, mode picks the smallest value, not the first one seen.

11
00:01:24,009 --> 00:01:30,716
Sum and average skip nulls too. The average divides by nine, the values that exist, not by ten.

12
00:01:31,516 --> 00:01:34,319
Median takes the middle of the sorted values.

13
00:01:35,119 --> 00:01:40,652
Now watch one big spender. The mean chases the outlier, while the median barely moves.

14
00:01:41,452 --> 00:01:44,361
Weighted mean averages with a weight on each row.

15
00:01:45,161 --> 00:01:51,334
Min, max and range describe the edges. One extreme value can stretch the range on its own.

16
00:01:52,134 --> 00:02:00,590
Variance and standard deviation use the population formula. They divide by n, describing these rows exactly as they are.

17
00:02:01,390 --> 00:02:11,339
Welford divides by n minus one, the sample formula, and returns the mean, the variance and the count together. Tests and t overlays read that triple.

18
00:02:12,139 --> 00:02:16,435
Same rows, two different variances. Use the one your method expects.

19
00:02:17,235 --> 00:02:24,134
The confidence-interval bounds put a range around the mean: the mean, plus or minus z times the standard error.

20
00:02:24,934 --> 00:02:35,907
Leave the big spender out and let the rows fall: eight, four, two. Fewer rows give a wider interval. With a single row there's no variance to estimate, so both bounds are null.

21
00:02:36,707 --> 00:02:43,584
Pulse uses the normal z, not Student's t, so very small samples read narrower than a t interval would.

22
00:02:44,384 --> 00:02:50,685
Spread widens it too. Put the big spender back, and all nine rows give the widest interval on screen.

23
00:02:51,485 --> 00:02:58,298
Ratio divides one field's sum by another's. A row with a null on either side is left out of both sums.

24
00:02:59,098 --> 00:03:03,863
Because it pools the sums first, bigger rows count for more. That's a pooled rate.

25
00:03:04,663 --> 00:03:09,599
Averaging each row's ratio answers a different question, and gives a different number.

26
00:03:10,399 --> 00:03:16,274
If the denominators sum to zero, the ratio is null. It is never an error and never zero.

27
00:03:17,074 --> 00:03:23,653
Accuracy aggregators compare an actual field with a prediction field, often a regression prediction attribute.

28
00:03:24,453 --> 00:03:33,293
Mean absolute error is the average miss, in the field's own units. Root mean squared error squares the misses first, so big ones dominate.

29
00:03:34,093 --> 00:03:42,250
Mean absolute percentage error is a fraction, so point zero five means five percent. Rows missing either side are skipped.

30
00:03:43,050 --> 00:03:52,381
The area under the curve measures ranking instead. It takes a score and a zero or one outcome, and asks how often a renewer outranks a non-renewer.

31
00:03:53,181 --> 00:03:58,330
Two members share a score of point six four, one on each side. A tie counts half.

32
00:03:59,130 --> 00:04:08,183
Here it reads point seven eight. The components add the Gini, the count on each side, and a DeLong interval. It ranks; it does not calibrate.

33
00:04:08,983 --> 00:04:12,042
Shape aggregators describe how values spread out.

34
00:04:12,842 --> 00:04:17,991
Skewness is positive when a long tail stretches to the right, like most spending data.

35
00:04:18,791 --> 00:04:25,242
Kurtosis is excess kurtosis, so a normal curve reads zero. Both use population moments.

36
00:04:26,042 --> 00:04:30,658
Percentile cuts the sorted values at any point you choose, here the ninetieth.

37
00:04:31,458 --> 00:04:38,719
With one row or none, or no spread at all, skewness and kurtosis are null, never zero. There is no shape to see.

38
00:04:39,519 --> 00:04:46,460
Multi-select fields have their own aggregators. One member skipped the question, and one answered but picked nothing.

39
00:04:47,260 --> 00:04:54,713
Set frequency counts each option. A row can count toward several options, so the counts add up to more than the rows.

40
00:04:55,513 --> 00:05:06,273
Union is what anyone picked. Intersection is what everyone picked, so one empty answer empties it. Cardinality and distinct values count picks and combinations.

41
00:05:07,073 --> 00:05:10,964
An empty selection is a real answer. Only null is skipped.

42
00:05:11,764 --> 00:05:20,668
Shards and streaming chunks are folded separately, then merged. Most aggregators keep a small, fixed running state, so merging is instant.

43
00:05:21,468 --> 00:05:27,535
Partial aggregators keep a map of values, such as distinct counts and modes, and merge it at the end.

44
00:05:28,335 --> 00:05:36,919
Median, percentile and the area under the curve need every value at once. They buffer the rows and sort, so memory grows with the cohort.

45
00:05:37,719 --> 00:05:43,508
The manifest lists each aggregator's class, and predict flags the buffered ones before you run.

46
00:05:44,308 --> 00:05:49,564
Rows can carry weights, set on the request, on one slot, or as an instance default.

47
00:05:50,364 --> 00:05:56,409
A weighted count becomes a sum of weights, and a weighted average is the weighted sum over the sum of weights.

48
00:05:57,209 --> 00:06:04,150
Setting weight to null opts one slot out. Invalid weights are left out with a warning, and n stays a raw count.

49
00:06:04,950 --> 00:06:10,462
Every aggregation also reports its components: the pieces its figure was made from.

50
00:06:11,262 --> 00:06:19,803
Every one starts with the same floor: n, the values used, and n null, the values skipped. Above it are the aggregator's own parts.

51
00:06:20,603 --> 00:06:24,600
You can switch components off. The figures themselves never change.

52
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:36,011
That's the aggregator toolkit today: counting, centre and spread, uncertainty, shape, accuracy and ranking, multi-select, and vectors that give one figure per member.

53
00:06:36,811 --> 00:06:44,755
More aggregators are added over time, and embedders can register their own. The manifest always shows what your instance offers.

54
00:06:45,555 --> 00:06:49,275
Remember: read n beside every figure. Next up, windows.

55
00:06:50,075 --> 00:06:51,555
Pulse aggregators.
